  "query_preview": "..\nSince sunrise earlier today, there has been isolated showers \nand storms, mainly across the high terrain. High clouds and\nsmoke at high levels has took the edge off the heating some\nearlier today.\n\nExpect activity to weaken over the next few hours. The cold\nfront is still well to the north and west. Best area for storms\nto fire up late is well to the west of central PA, as noted by\nSPC day one outlk.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Severe thunderstorms capable of damaging winds \nand heavy rainfall remain possible on Tuesday.\n\nComplex pattern for the next few days. As been the case since\nearly Spring, models close off the low by later Wed. A winter \nlike pattern. Also one that supports heavy rain not too far\nfrom our CWA.\n\nBefore this time, models such as the 12Z NAM drop the activity\nto the south of our area on Tuesday. Short range models show\na lot of variation. Anything forming to our west late tonight\nwould likely have to drop more to the south, then to the east\nor northeast. \n\nMore information below.\n\nTiming and placement uncertainty grows heading into tonight \nwith continued CAM variability concerning downstream \ndevelopment/propagation of Great Lakes/Ohio Valley MCS activity.\nThe main shortwave does appear to reach CPA by Tuesday morning.\nThe decaying? MCS or related feature and associated cloud cover\ncould dampen severe risk into the afternoon. Hires guidance \nseems to suggest an eastward shift in the greatest svr/FF risk \nas the trough deepens over the northern Mid Atlantic. Some heavy\nrain potential will linger into Wednesday as the slow moving \nupper low pivots from northeast PA toward southern New England. \nStorm totals could exceed 2-3 over some parts of the northeast \nCWA by Thursday morning.",
